#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2c90d8-0f2e-5f84-bf67-7620d6f1d308" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Design Matrix When the Regression Curve Contains Zero Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "98ec57bb-04f5-559a-9057-854de19eda7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5612" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072333/assets/fe5d2bbb69e110d08d3231d486af483dc05d995e30907a5f9a62cf46743e81d7.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ | $\\color{red}6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133539 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f92926-0c15-554a-8fba-55b05d435980" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for a Least-Squares Regression Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f5354fe5-fa4b-5443-a097-40174b0b7b16" :question/math-academy-id "e-5613" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression (in terms of $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$) for the vector of coefficients $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ of the curve $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072333/assets/0329ad2c30d00d6051e2be094d076960d3ecfc35f9054feac195787f3af7328d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\nX \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model"}] :question/id #uuid "75c0086b-db74-5f01-8d66-f023dd7b575d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6094" 